Load decks of sp_mos1 against ngspice-45, written by ../load.py mos1.
Builds on PATH: ngspice-45 jfngv2gd3zvi5cmr9vrqfhnziipqd09n (source hfvpfb0bgw9819wkb7kzpdaasbd0hd5r, devices/mos1),
VACASK dhxz6l3iac3r2b8n91kcz04a0j3hs4s6 (devices/spice/mos1.va, ngspice-47-159 vadng-pre-48).
Run: `nix develop .#testing -c tests/fixtures/families/load_run.sh mos1`. i(vd) in A, |i(vd)| at 1 GHz for acg/acd.
nom is 27 C, m=1, the control of m3 (27 C, m=3) and of off (80 C, m=3); all give w, l, ad, as, pd, ps, a bulk at -0.5 V,
junctions (cj, cjsw, js) and Meyer capacitances (tox, cgso, cgdo, cgbo). Regions (vgs, vds): cut (0.3, 2), lin (3, 0.2),
sat (1.5, 3).
Oracles (fd.py, last lines): each nominal region's operating point moved 1e-5 V on the gate (base) and the drain
(collector, anode), whose central differences are gm and gds of the DC law, against |i(vd)| at 1 kHz; it holds where
the conductance is well above the 1 kHz capacitive current and the differences' resolution (ngspice prints 12 digits:
about 5e-11 S), so not in cutoff or reverse. And the multiplier's metamorphic relation: every m3 reading is 3 times its
nom reading within 1e-9 relative and 1e-10 absolute (the solver's gmin is not multiplied); a failing row is printed.
Result: no difference. The capacitances are read: nom_cut_acg is w*cgdo (3.0e-15 F at 1 GHz), nom_cut_acd w*cgdo
plus the reverse-biased drain junction (1.09e-14 F). The cutoff currents (gmin across the drain junction plus js*ad)
stand 2.6e-17 A apart at abstol 1e-15 (2.96e-17 A with js=1, a current 1e4 times larger: a constant, not the junction
law); at VACASK's default abstol nom_cut_dc reads -2.693e-12 against -2.502e-12 (the solver's tolerance).

